{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DnvLm8vEGSuBZpaGrov1as","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t+++\n2\tid = \"WI-572\"\n3\ttitle = \"The approval act is the adjudicator's, on trunk: lanes author Drafted rows, a first-approval adjudication flips and snapshots them\"\n4\tworkstream = \"process\"\n5\tneeds = [\"WI-571\"]\n6\tspecref = \"\"\n7\tbuildtier = \"strong\"\n8\tpriority = 5\n9\tsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\n10\t+++\n11\t\n12\t## Deliverable\n13\t\n14\tThe approval act on a spine row — the `Status` flip into `Approved`/`Founded`\n15\tand the `docs/archive/last_approved/` copy that anchors it — is now the\n16\tadjudicator's, on the serial trunk side. A worker lane authors `Drafted` rows\n17\tand amends cell text; nothing else changed about what a lane may do.\n18\t\n19\t1. **Lanes cannot approve.** The merge slot REFUSES a work branch whose spine\n20\t   delta flips a `Status`, mints a row born claiming approval, or writes the\n21\t   snapshot directory — by name, listing each row, its registry and every file\n22\t   touched — each worded by what the branch DID to it — with the remedy.\n23\t   Construction-first: `staged_approval_acts` reports the set\n24\t   `staged_spine_amendments` exempts MINUS the de-approvals (a withdrawal\n25\t   blesses nothing, and `staged_drafted_rows` raises the re-approval it owes\n26\t   instead), off one shared two-tree walk (`_spine_row_sides`), so no second\n27\t   detector entered. The judgement is\n28\t   `acceptance_record.lane_approval_refusal`; `integrate._approval_act_refusal`\n29\t   is the rung. Verified against the record: the reader reproduces the\n30\t   pre-ruling census exactly — the four flips at `580df781` and the\n31\t   born-`Approved` rows of WI-483, WI-500, WI-501, WI-507.\n32\t2. **A first-approval adjudication arm exists.** Trigger (a2) mints ONE\n33\t   `brief = \"first-approval\"` row per merge over the `Drafted` rows the lane\n34\t   handed over — including a status-only withdrawal into `Drafted` — on the\n35\t   rungs the dial releases; a held rung is not minted and\n36\t   surfaces to the owner as before. Those row ids are RECORDED on the minted\n37\t   row (`Adjudicates`, a new `wi_convert` column) and the brief — which\n38\t   re-derives its population live, because the row is claimed long after the\n39\t   merge — acts only on the intersection, so the act cannot widen past what the\n40\t   merge handed over. Its brief renders each row's WHOLE chain\n41\t   (`trace.spine_chain`, extracted from `reattest_model` so the judge and the\n42\t   owner's surface cannot disagree) and asks one question — approve, or return\n43\t   with findings — with the `--approves` argument of the approving commit\n44\t   derived rather than typed — rendered through\n45\t   `baseline_snapshot.format_approves`, the deterministic inverse of\n46\t   `parse_approves`, so the module that CONSUMES the `;`-joined list is the only\n47\t   one that names the separator; a batch spanning two registries cannot\n48\t   mis-parse into a second registry that is neither authorised nor copied. Its\n49\t   verdict grammar is its own. The adjudication lane is the permitted actor,\n50\t   but not a blanket exemption: the merge slot derives its approval delta once,\n51\t   joins every flip to the claimed row's `Adjudicates` scope, and admits exactly\n52\t   the snapshots of registries holding those flips. An empty scope, an unrelated\n53\t   flip, a missing anchor or a registry-widening copy refuses before merge.\n54\t   `_adjudication_lane` decides the actor off the same\n55\t   `_claimed_spec_frontmatters` read the bar directive uses, and the\n56\t   adjudication already runs ALONE\n57\t   (`dispatch._branch_exclusive`), which is the concurrency guarantee the ruling\n58\t   points the act at rather than a new mechanism.\n59\t3. **The amendment arm's aftermath is stated and true.** The stale \"the flip is\n60\t   the mechanical tool's act, not yours\" — false since OI-45 (b) retired that\n61\t   tool — is replaced by a DERIVED `{aftermath}` slot that reads the declared\n62\t   gate authority for the tiers shown and tells the session whether the\n63\t   re-attestation is its own act or the owner's.\n64\t4. **The doctrine says it once:** PROCESS.md §4's fixed points, linking to\n65\t   PROCESS_OPTIONS.md \"Who performs the approval act\" (the ruling, its two\n66\t   reasons, the division-of-labour table, the three holding mechanisms); OI-45\n67\t   (b) narrowed by one sentence; `gate-advance`, `spine-authoring` and\n68\t   `worker.template.md` updated. **And it says only what the rung reaches.**\n69\t   The act is scoped to SPINE rows — SN/SR/LLR/TC, all four tiers\n70\t   (`acceptance_record.APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS`; the SN tier joined at REVIEW-A round\n71\t   028, because a need is a spine row and `DevStg-Reqs` is the rung the dial\n72\t   holds) — which is the ruling's own scope, so the doctrine names that bound\n73\t   rather than stating an unqualified ban the merge slot does not enforce. The\n74\t   bound is CLOSED, not merely declared:\n75\t   `acceptance_record.OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT` names the three OFF-SPINE\n76\t   snapshotted registries, whose approval cells are OI-30 D3's, and the two\n77\t   lists are pinned exhaustive and disjoint against\n78\t   `baseline_snapshot.SNAPSHOTTED`, so a tier added to the snapshot must land on\n79\t   one side by a deliberate edit and cannot reach no approval reader at all.\n80\t   The two generators that tell a human signer what a snapshot copies were\n81\t   corrected to WI-571's scoped statement, which PROCESS.md §4 already made.\n82\t5. **Tests** in the modules' existing style: seven original merge-slot cases\n83\t   (both `_adjudication_lane` actor-classification arms among them) plus six\n84\t   scope cases at that trust boundary (permitted scoped act, empty scope,\n85\t   actor-kind-only flip, return-only batch, unanchored flip and widened\n86\t   snapshot), four at the\n87\t   reader, four at the trigger, ten at the brief, one at the\n88\t   `format_approves`/`parse_approves` round trip — including the pin that the\n89\t   brief's terminal sequence cannot stop before performing an approved row's\n90\t   flip-and-snapshot act, and the mutation-proven regression that an act cannot\n91\t   reach a row the merge did not hand it. The schema pin now covers the READ\n92\t   side's copy of the column list too, and `test_wi_loader_sync` carries the\n93\t   scope cell as the third member of its `bar`/`brief` triplet.\n94\t\n95\tThe approving commit's own COMMAND LINE is safe to run, which the derived\n96\targument alone did not make it. The `;`-joined `--approves` value is quoted\n97\twhere it is rendered into a shell — unquoted, a two-registry batch was two\n98\tcommands, anchoring half the act — pinned by a `shlex` guard that distinguishes\n99\ta quoted `;` from a separator where `shlex.split` cannot. And because\n100\t`{registries}` is fixed at composition while the approve/return split exists\n101\tonly after the verdict, the brief now derives WHICH ROWS each token covers, the\n102\ttemplate states the drop rule for a mixed batch, and the merge refusal names a\n103\tremedy per arm.\n104\t\n105\tTwo repairs the close itself unmasked rode this row rather than a follow-up,\n106\tbecause the bar fails where it surfaces: `wi_convert.read_specs` now walks the\n107\tfolder home through the READ side's own `spec_paths` (a tracked `README.md`\n108\tunder a status directory was a broken row to the writer and residue to the\n109\treader), and the four `test_wi_convert.py` guards that a catch-all\n110\t`ConvertError` skip had kept dark since WI-504 run again.\n111\t\n112\tDeviations, the ratchet re-stamps, and the one follow-on this row deliberately\n113\tdoes not take (narrowing the owner's approval brief to the held rungs) are in\n114\t`docs/log.d/WI-572-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md`.\n115\t\n116\t## Context\n117\t\n118\tFiled 2026-09-01 (evening supervised session) from the owner's ruling made in session (docs/log.d/2026-09-01-owner-ruling-approval-act.md, compiled): approval acts on spine rows - the Status flip and the anchoring snapshot - are the adjudicator's alone, on the serial trunk side, for whole-chain context and for concurrency. A lane's merge is refused on any flip, born-Approved row or snapshot write; a first-approval adjudication arm is minted at merge with the whole chain in its brief and flips + snapshots on trunk; the amendment arm's stale mechanical-tool line is replaced by the true aftermath; the doctrine is stated once. Serialized behind the copy-scope row (both touch intake.py and baseline_snapshot.py). Read the plan's section 2 before widening.\n119\t\n120\t**Standing constraint (the ruling this row executes):** if this row authors\n121\tor amends spine rows (SR/LLR/TC), leave them `Drafted`; do NOT flip any\n122\t`Status`, and do NOT run `intake.py snapshot` or write\n123\t`docs/archive/last_approved/` on this lane — drive the new refusal and the\n124\tadjudication arm on a scaffold. The first-approval adjudication this row\n125\tships is what performs the flip and the snapshot, on trunk.\n126\t\n127\tHonoured: this lane moved no `Status` and wrote no snapshot; every arm is driven\n128\ton a scaffold repo built by the tests. It amended three rows its own code made\n129\tstale — `LLR-158`'s `code_symbol`/`Detail` (left `Approved`, the drift being the\n130\tsignal), `LLR-136`'s `Detail` (also left `Approved`; the `wi_convert.read_specs`\n131\trepair) and `IF-091`'s requestors (already `Drafted`) — which the constraint\n132\tpermits: a lane amends, an adjudicator approves. Checked against this row's own\n133\treader rather than assumed — `lane_approval_refusal` returns `None` over the\n134\tbranch delta, and `staged_spine_amendments` reports `LLR-136` and `LLR-158`, so\n135\tthe amendment adjudication is raised for them at merge.\n136\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13e3d284-9cb2-46f5-9233-24a0a1319ae2","uuid":"7e348ce4-b306-40e3-9d68-a61159976c11","timestamp":"2026-09-02T10:50:24.672Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/Documents/ai-template-drive/wi-574-spot-check-the-clean-close-of/docs/archive/work/complete/WI-572-the-approval-act-is-the-adjudi.md","content":"+++\nid = \"WI-572\"\ntitle = \"The approval act is the adjudicator's, on trunk: lanes author Drafted rows, a first-approval adjudication flips and snapshots them\"\nworkstream = \"process\"\nneeds = [\"WI-571\"]\nspecref = \"\"\nbuildtier = \"strong\"\npriority = 5\nsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\n+++\n\n## Deliverable\n\nThe approval act on a spine row — the `Status` flip into `Approved`/`Founded`\nand the `docs/archive/last_approved/` copy that anchors it — is now the\nadjudicator's, on the serial trunk side. A worker lane authors `Drafted` rows\nand amends cell text; nothing else changed about what a lane may do.\n\n1. **Lanes cannot approve.** The merge slot REFUSES a work branch whose spine\n   delta flips a `Status`, mints a row born claiming approval, or writes the\n   snapshot directory — by name, listing each row, its registry and every file\n   touched — each worded by what the branch DID to it — with the remedy.\n   Construction-first: `staged_approval_acts` reports the set\n   `staged_spine_amendments` exempts MINUS the de-approvals (a withdrawal\n   blesses nothing, and `staged_drafted_rows` raises the re-approval it owes\n   instead), off one shared two-tree walk (`_spine_row_sides`), so no second\n   detector entered. The judgement is\n   `acceptance_record.lane_approval_refusal`; `integrate._approval_act_refusal`\n   is the rung. Verified against the record: the reader reproduces the\n   pre-ruling census exactly — the four flips at `580df781` and the\n   born-`Approved` rows of WI-483, WI-500, WI-501, WI-507.\n2. **A first-approval adjudication arm exists.** Trigger (a2) mints ONE\n   `brief = \"first-approval\"` row per merge over the `Drafted` rows the lane\n   handed over — including a status-only withdrawal into `Drafted` — on the\n   rungs the dial releases; a held rung is not minted and\n   surfaces to the owner as before. Those row ids are RECORDED on the minted\n   row (`Adjudicates`, a new `wi_convert` column) and the brief — which\n   re-derives its population live, because the row is claimed long after the\n   merge — acts only on the intersection, so the act cannot widen past what the\n   merge handed over. Its brief renders each row's WHOLE chain\n   (`trace.spine_chain`, extracted from `reattest_model` so the judge and the\n   owner's surface cannot disagree) and asks one question — approve, or return\n   with findings — with the `--approves` argument of the approving commit\n   derived rather than typed — rendered through\n   `baseline_snapshot.format_approves`, the deterministic inverse of\n   `parse_approves`, so the module that CONSUMES the `;`-joined list is the only\n   one that names the separator; a batch spanning two registries cannot\n   mis-parse into a second registry that is neither authorised nor copied. Its\n   verdict grammar is its own. The adjudication lane is the permitted actor,\n   but not a blanket exemption: the merge slot derives its approval delta once,\n   joins every flip to the claimed row's `Adjudicates` scope, and admits exactly\n   the snapshots of registries holding those flips. An empty scope, an unrelated\n   flip, a missing anchor or a registry-widening copy refuses before merge.\n   `_adjudication_lane` decides the actor off the same\n   `_claimed_spec_frontmatters` read the bar directive uses, and the\n   adjudication already runs ALONE\n   (`dispatch._branch_exclusive`), which is the concurrency guarantee the ruling\n   points the act at rather than a new mechanism.\n3. **The amendment arm's aftermath is stated and true.** The stale \"the flip is\n   the mechanical tool's act, not yours\" — false since OI-45 (b) retired that\n   tool — is replaced by a DERIVED `{aftermath}` slot that reads the declared\n   gate authority for the tiers shown and tells the session whether the\n   re-attestation is its own act or the owner's.\n4. **The doctrine says it once:** PROCESS.md §4's fixed points, linking to\n   PROCESS_OPTIONS.md \"Who performs the approval act\" (the ruling, its two\n   reasons, the division-of-labour table, the three holding mechanisms); OI-45\n   (b) narrowed by one sentence; `gate-advance`, `spine-authoring` and\n   `worker.template.md` updated. **And it says only what the rung reaches.**\n   The act is scoped to SPINE rows — SN/SR/LLR/TC, all four tiers\n   (`acceptance_record.APPROVAL_ACT_CSVS`; the SN tier joined at REVIEW-A round\n   028, because a need is a spine row and `DevStg-Reqs` is the rung the dial\n   holds) — which is the ruling's own scope, so the doctrine names that bound\n   rather than stating an unqualified ban the merge slot does not enforce. The\n   bound is CLOSED, not merely declared:\n   `acceptance_record.OUTSIDE_THE_APPROVAL_ACT` names the three OFF-SPINE\n   snapshotted registries, whose approval cells are OI-30 D3's, and the two\n   lists are pinned exhaustive and disjoint against\n   `baseline_snapshot.SNAPSHOTTED`, so a tier added to the snapshot must land on\n   one side by a deliberate edit and cannot reach no approval reader at all.\n   The two generators that tell a human signer what a snapshot copies were\n   corrected to WI-571's scoped statement, which PROCESS.md §4 already made.\n5. **Tests** in the modules' existing style: seven original merge-slot cases\n   (both `_adjudication_lane` actor-classification arms among them) plus six\n   scope cases at that trust boundary (permitted scoped act, empty scope,\n   actor-kind-only flip, return-only batch, unanchored flip and widened\n   snapshot), four at the\n   reader, four at the trigger, ten at the brief, one at the\n   `format_approves`/`parse_approves` round trip — including the pin that the\n   brief's terminal sequence cannot stop before performing an approved row's\n   flip-and-snapshot act, and the mutation-proven regression that an act cannot\n   reach a row the merge did not hand it. The schema pin now covers the READ\n   side's copy of the column list too, and `test_wi_loader_sync` carries the\n   scope cell as the third member of its `bar`/`brief` triplet.\n\nThe approving commit's own COMMAND LINE is safe to run, which the derived\nargument alone did not make it. The `;`-joined `--approves` value is quoted\nwhere it is rendered into a shell — unquoted, a two-registry batch was two\ncommands, anchoring half the act — pinned by a `shlex` guard that distinguishes\na quoted `;` from a separator where `shlex.split` cannot. And because\n`{registries}` is fixed at composition while the approve/return split exists\nonly after the verdict, the brief now derives WHICH ROWS each token covers, the\ntemplate states the drop rule for a mixed batch, and the merge refusal names a\nremedy per arm.\n\nTwo repairs the close itself unmasked rode this row rather than a follow-up,\nbecause the bar fails where it surfaces: `wi_convert.read_specs` now walks the\nfolder home through the READ side's own `spec_paths` (a tracked `README.md`\nunder a status directory was a broken row to the writer and residue to the\nreader), and the four `test_wi_convert.py` guards that a catch-all\n`ConvertError` skip had kept dark since WI-504 run again.\n\nDeviations, the ratchet re-stamps, and the one follow-on this row deliberately\ndoes not take (narrowing the owner's approval brief to the held rungs) are in\n`docs/log.d/WI-572-approval-act-adjudicator-only.md`.\n\n## Context\n\nFiled 2026-09-01 (evening supervised session) from the owner's ruling made in session (docs/log.d/2026-09-01-owner-ruling-approval-act.md, compiled): approval acts on spine rows - the Status flip and the anchoring snapshot - are the adjudicator's alone, on the serial trunk side, for whole-chain context and for concurrency. A lane's merge is refused on any flip, born-Approved row or snapshot write; a first-approval adjudication arm is minted at merge with the whole chain in its brief and flips + snapshots on trunk; the amendment arm's stale mechanical-tool line is replaced by the true aftermath; the doctrine is stated once. Serialized behind the copy-scope row (both touch intake.py and baseline_snapshot.py). Read the plan's section 2 before widening.\n\n**Standing constraint (the ruling this row executes):** if this row authors\nor amends spine rows (SR/LLR/TC), leave them `Drafted`; do NOT flip any\n`Status`, and do NOT run `intake.py snapshot` or write\n`docs/archive/last_approved/` on this lane — drive the new refusal and the\nadjudication arm on a scaffold. The first-approval adjudication this row\nships is what performs the flip and the snapshot, on trunk.\n\nHonoured: this lane moved no `Status` and wrote no snapshot; every arm is driven\non a scaffold repo built by the tests. It amended three rows its own code made\nstale — `LLR-158`'s `code_symbol`/`Detail` (left `Approved`, the drift being the\nsignal), `LLR-136`'s `Detail` (also left `Approved`; the `wi_convert.read_specs`\nrepair) and `IF-091`'s requestors (already `Drafted`) — which the constraint\npermits: a lane amends, an adjudicator approves. Checked against this row's own\nreader rather than assumed — `lane_approval_refusal` returns `None` over the\nbranch delta, and `staged_spine_amendments` reports `LLR-136` and `LLR-158`, so\nthe amendment adjudication is raised for them at merge.\n","numLines":136,"startLine":1,"totalLines":136}}}
